<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What Android's automatic backup carries, on Android 11 and below.

  THIS IS THE APP'S REAL BACKUP, and it costs no code, no permission, no
  sign-in and no OAuth client. Android copies the app's private storage to the
  user's Google account roughly once a day while the phone is idle, charging
  and on wi-fi, then restores it when they set up a new phone or reinstall.
  It is the mechanism people already understand from their chat app, and the
  farmer never has to know it is happening.

  The ledger is a SQLite file under databases/, which is included by default.
  It is listed explicitly anyway: a default that nobody wrote down is a default
  somebody removes by accident, and this file is the entire farm.

  Excluded on purpose:

    sharedpref/CapacitorStorage — holds the chosen language and other device
    preferences. Harmless to carry, but restoring a WebView's own scratch
    state onto different hardware is the kind of thing that produces a bug
    nobody can reproduce. The farmer re-picking Kannada once is cheaper.

    cache — reports and temporary files, regenerated on demand. Backing up a
    PDF nobody asked for eats the 25 MB quota that the ledger needs.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <include domain="database" path="." />
    <include domain="file" path="." />

    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="CapacitorStorage.xml" />
    <exclude domain="cache" path="." />
    <exclude domain="external_cache" path="." />
</full-backup-content>
